A||N||7||btklassy||86 v6 SE stalled||||1||
Z||000000||btklassy||12-11-2001||06:24 PM||||I replaced my ignition module and started driving my Fiero to work. A week later, after driving for 6 blocks I lost power so I pulled over and the engine died. <P>I restarted it and it died. And again. Then it would crank without starting. I waited about 5 minutes and it started normally and I drove it back home and parked it. I haven't tried to move it since. <P>I would like to change the fuel filter as a first step. I am unable to get the fittings loose. Even after spraying them for two days they won't budge. I'm afraid to apply too much torque and end up damaging the fittings. Is there a slick way to get this puppy off. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Devotshka||12-11-2001||06:32 PM||||Just curious, did you use and aftermarket ignition module?  I've never had an aftermarket module last more than 3 weeks or so, you really should get GM/Delco for that part, in my experience, aftermarket ones behave just like yours does after a few weeks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||12-11-2001||06:55 PM||||For what it's worth, I have owned a 1986 V6 Fiero SE since new, it still starts instantly, and its ignition module has <B>never</B> been replaced.  I think that says the original GM module is a pretty good unit.  Not everyone has been so lucky, apparently.<P>I can't comment on the quality of any of the aftermarket modules.  But stories about bad luck with aftermarket modules have been repeated here often enough, I am beginning to believe them.   <P>All I can recommend for getting the fuel filter off is:  Soak with Liquid Wrench, use a line wrench on the fitting, and support the filter (i.e. - keep it from twisting) with a Crescent Wrench.  Take it easy, and try not to twist the lines.   Of course at this point, you may decide you don't want to mess with the filter at all. <P>BTW, I only changed the filter <B>once</B> on the aforementioned car, and <B>that</B> was mainly so that I could drain its fuel tank, prior to having it shipped cross country.         ||||reg||1
Z||000003||btklassy||12-11-2001||07:30 PM||||I did use an after ignition module. I guess the lifetime warranty is worth much if I have to replace it every 2 weeks. <P>I am not convinced that the new ignition module failed already. I would expect it to fail because of heat but not much heat could have built up in 6 blocks. I did use the heat dissipation goo that came with it. <P>By the way, what is a line wrench for getting my fuel filter off?||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTDude||12-11-2001||09:35 PM||||Only aftermarket modules i've had any luck with are echlin from NAPA.  Not the cheap line NAPA!  The ECHLIN.  Installed countless and can only remember one failure.  That's a pretty good record.  A line wrench looks much like a boxed end wrench with the top section cut out to allow the line to pass thru it.  This give you more gripping power on the nut.  Never discount the value of a large set of vice grip pliers tho.  They may take it off when the line wrench doesn't.<P>GTDude||||reg||1
Z||000005||NEPTUNE||12-12-2001||09:45 AM||||Have you tested the coil?<P>I had an aftermarket module last 3 years. Others lasted 3 months. Your parts store can probably test it for you. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||12-12-2001||08:53 PM||||I'd have to say either the module,or maybe some bad gas,did the car sit for awhile before this?||||reg||1
Z||000007||btklassy||12-13-2001||05:57 PM||||It was sitting for a month or more. <P>I thought if the ignition module was bad that it would stay bad. I don't think heat is an issue. If the module went bad I would think that 5 minutes later it wouldn't work normally again...||||reg||1
